name=biteprank
version=1.3
desc=Forges a prank link that opens as something innocent, then goes off on their screen
category=mischief
author=GLITCH-BITE-404
exec=run
preview=--preview
deps=
usage=biteprank   ·   biteprank list, biteprank <scene>, biteprank make -s ransom -n Yuval -b video -H tunnel
keys=ESC:ends any scene instantly, on their end|-b:what the link looks like — video, photo, folder, none|-H:how it is sent — tunnel, local, github, file|list:every scene and what it does|list --full:the long version|about:how it works and what it refuses to do|make:forge one with every option spelled out|Ctrl-C:takes the link down (local and tunnel)
about=A prank generator, not a prank runner — nothing here touches your friend's machine, because a browser tab can't. You pick a scene, it forges one self-contained .html (every byte inlined, every sound synthesised on the spot) and then hands you a link to send. The link does not open on a wall of red text: it opens as something ordinary — a message with a blurred video and a play button, a photo waiting to be revealed, a shared folder with three believable files — and the scene erupts out of whatever they press. That disguise is not decoration. A browser refuses fullscreen and refuses to make any sound until the visitor has deliberately clicked something, so the bait is what earns the one click that makes everything else work. The scenes that imitate an operating system are built to match it rather than to look cool: the stop screen is Segoe UI on the real blue with the QR block beside the support text, format is an elevated Command Prompt with the actual build banner and format.com's own capitalised warning, the update screen uses the five-dot Windows spinner, and the kernel panic is bare grey-on-black console with no filter over it at all. The CRT scanline overlay is opt-in for exactly that reason — a scanlined BSOD is a poster of a BSOD. Fifteen scenes. trace is the flagship: it prints their real public address, the city it resolves to, their carrier, their browser, their screen and their exact battery percentage, and once those true things land the fake 4.2 GB upload underneath is believed too. No exploit, just theatre with a few honest sentences in it. There is a kernel panic whose call trace has a frame called trust_a_stranger, a blue screen naming their actual browser as the failing module, an update screen that sticks at 99% for six seconds then reverts, forty-five wandering cursors, a battery that drains from wherever theirs really sits, and bitten — the no-scare one that shows the same real fields and closes by pointing out every site they visit can already read all of it. Send it by public cloudflared tunnel, over your own Wi-Fi, or to GitHub Pages; whichever tools that needs, it offers to install when you pick it instead of refusing to start. With a tunnel or a Wi-Fi link you also get a live feed in your terminal — opened the link, took the bait, reached the GOTCHA — so you watch it land instead of wondering. Lines it will not cross: no bait or scene ever asks for a password, a login or a payment, none of them imitate a real company, nothing about the visitor is stored anywhere, and ESC ends any scene immediately. Every scene resolves to the same reveal — a big GOTCHA and a plain sentence saying nothing happened and nothing was taken — because a prank someone cannot escape, or cannot tell was a prank afterwards, is just being mean to somebody. Three scenes are marked spicy; aim those carefully.
